:[{"text":"உட்கும்","lemma":"உட்கு","gloss":"fearing"}],"gloss":"fearing"},{"surface":"தானைக்","parts":[{"text":"தானை","lemma":"தானை","gloss":"army"},{"text":"க்","lemma":"க்","gloss":"sandhi"}],"gloss":"army"}]},{"words":[{"surface":"கெடல்அருங்","parts":[{"text":"கெடல்அருங்","lemma":"கெடல்அருங்","gloss":"difficult to ruin"}],"gloss":"difficult to ruin","split_unverified":true},{"surface":"குறைய","parts":[{"text":"குறை","lemma":"குறை","gloss":"need/request"},{"text":"அ","lemma":"அ","gloss":"suffix"}],"gloss":"request"},{"surface":"நாடுகிழ","parts":[{"text":"நாடு","lemma":"நாடு","gloss":"country"},{"text":"கிழ","lemma":"கிழ","gloss":"lord"}],"gloss":"lord of the country"},{"surface":"வோயே","parts":[{"text":"வோயே","lemma":"வோயே","gloss":"O you!"}],"gloss":"O you!","split_unverified":true}]}],"literal":"\"Who are these?\" if you ask, these indeed / along with their town, to the beggars, having bestowed, along with their chariot, / to the jasmine plant, gave, unfading good fame, / having sounding bells, of the elephant-riding Parambu hill's lord, / tall and great Pari's daughters, I indeed / father's friend, these are my daughters, / the Brahmin poet, having brought, came indeed. / You indeed, in the northern sage's sacrificial pot, having appeared, / with copper, having crafted, the high and long fort, / without getting tired of giving, Tuvarai, having ruled, / forty-nine generations, having come, / among the Velir, O chieftain! victorious in war, O lord! / wearing a garland, having elephants, O great Irungovel! / manly duty because of possessing, duty to bards having performed, / wearing a flourishing leaf-chaplet, O slayer of the tiger! / as I give, please accept them; sky-covering / vast ocean surrounded by this world's rare power, / gold-yielding great mountain's lord, O victorious spear! / enemies fearing army, / difficult to ruin request, O lord of the country!","translation":"\"If you ask, 'Who are these girls?', they are the daughters of Pari of the tall, dark mountain, the lord of Parambu hill where elephants wear sounding bells—the one of unfading fame who gave his chariot to a jasmine vine and bestowed his towns upon beggars. As for me, I am their father's friend, a Brahmin poet, who has brought them here. You, O great Irungovel, who appeared from the sacrificial pot of the northern sage, who ruled over Tuvarai with its high copper-crafted walls and never tired of giving, coming in a lineage of forty-nine generations, a victorious lord among the Velir chieftains! O slayer of the tiger, wearing a chaplet of flourishing leaves, who performs your duty to bards out of your noble manly duty! As I offer them to you, please accept them. O lord of the gold-yielding great mountains, possessing rare power in this world surrounded by the vast ocean, O leader of an army that terrifies your enemies, O lord of the country, please grant this request that must not fail!\"","modern":"\"இவர்கள் யார்?\" என்று நீ கேட்பாயானால், இவர்கள், இரவலர்களுக்குத் தம் ஊர்களையும், முல்லைக் கொடிக்குத் தம் தேரையும் வழங்கி அழியாத நற்புகழ் பெற்ற, ஒலிக்கின்ற மணிகளைக் கொண்ட யானைகளை உடைய பறம்பு மலையின் தலைவனான பாரியின் மகள்கள் ஆவர். நானோ, அவர்தம் தந்தையின் தோழனான அந்தணப் புலவன்; இவர்களை உன்னிடம் அழைத்து வந்துள்ளேன். வடதிசை முனிவரின் வேள்விக் குண்டத்தில் தோன்றி, செம்பினால் இயற்றப்பட்ட உயர்ந்த மதில்களைக் கொண்ட துவாரகையை ஆண்டு, நாற்பத்தொன்பது தலைமுறைகளாக வந்த வேளிர் குலத்துத் தலைவனே! வெற்றி தரும் போரைச் செய்யும் அண்ணலே! மாலை அணிந்த யானைகளை உடைய இருங்கோவேளே! ஆண்மைக்குரிய கடமையால் பாணர்களுக்குரிய கடமையைச் செய்யும், தழைக் கண்ணி அணிந்த, புலியை வென்ற மாலே! நான் தர இவர்களை நீ ஏற்றுக் கொள்வாயாக. கடல் சூழ்ந்த இவ்வுலகில் அரிய வலிமையும், பொன் விளையும் பெரிய மலைகளும் கொண்ட தலைவனே! பகைவர் அஞ்சும் படையையும், அழியாத நாட்டையும் உடையவனே, என் வேண்டுகோளை ஏற்பாயாக!","explanation":"This poem is spoken by the famous poet Kapilar to the Velir chieftain Irungovel. Following the death of his close friend, the generous patron Vel Pari, Kapilar took charge of Pari's unmarried daughters and sought suitable grooms among other chieftains. In this poem, Kapilar introduces the girls as the daughters of the legendary Pari, who was famous for his extreme generosity, such as giving his chariot to a jasmine vine. Kapilar then praises Irungovel's illustrious lineage, tracing it back forty-nine generations to an ancestor who emerged from a northern sage's sacrificial pot and ruled over Dwaraka (Tuvarai). Kapilar appeals to Irungovel's sense of duty and noble lineage, asking him to accept the daughters of Pari in marriage.","model":"gemini-3.5-flash"},"timing":{"male":[[0.0,0.4],[0.4,0.62],[0.7,1.29],[1.29,1.5],[1.5,1.8],[1.8,2.6],[2.6,2.7],[2.7,3.5],[3.5,3.7],[3.7,5.1],[5.1,5.3],[5.3,5.6],[5.6,6.0],[6.0,7.3],[7.3,7.4],[7.4,8.0],[8.0,8.08],[8.08,8.1],[8.1,9.8],[9.8,10.2],[10.2,10.3],[10.3,11.3],[11.3,11.6],[11.6,12.2],[12.2,12.3],[12.3,13.7],[13.7,13.8],[13.8,14.4],[14.4,14.6],[14.6,15.8],[15.8,16.2],[16.2,16.4],[16.4,16.7],[16.7,17.6],[18.4,19.0],[19.0,19.1],[19.1,19.4],[19.4,20.1],[20.1,20.3],[20.3,21.3],[21.3,21.4],[21.4,21.8],[21.8,22.1],[22.1,23.6],[23.6,24.0],[24.0,24.3],[24.3,24.6],[24.6,25.3],[25.3,25.7],[25.7,25.7],[25.7,26.5],[26.5,27.5],[27.5,27.5],[27.5,27.5],[27.5,27.5],[27.5,29.3],[29.3,29.3],[29.3,30.1],[30.1,30.4],[30.4,31.7],[31.7,32.0],[32.0,32.25],[32.25,32.33],[32.33,32.4],[32.4,33.4],[33.4,33.5],[33.5,33.8],[33.8,34.7],[35.56,36.26],[36.26,36.86],[36.86,36.96],[36.96,37.46],[37.46,38.96],[38.96,39.06],[39.06,39.66],[39.66,40.36],[40.36,40.86],[40.86,41.46],[41.46,41.66],[41.66,43.06],[43.06,43.26],[43.26,43.59],[43.59,43.76]],"female":[[0.0,0.5],[0.5,0.73],[0.8,1.1],[1.25,1.7],[1.7,1.93],[2.0,2.8],[2.8,3.0],[3.0,3.9],[3.9,4.1],[4.1,5.6],[5.6,5.7],[5.7,6.1],[6.1,6.5],[6.5,7.7],[7.7,7.8],[7.8,8.5],[8.5,8.58],[8.58,8.6],[8.6,10.4],[10.4,10.9],[10.9,10.9],[10.9,12.0],[12.0,12.3],[12.3,12.8],[12.8,12.9],[12.9,14.4],[14.4,14.6],[14.6,15.2],[15.2,15.5],[15.5,16.7],[16.7,17.1],[17.1,17.3],[17.3,17.7],[17.7,18.7],[19.4,20.0],[20.0,20.2],[20.2,20.5]